abstract:
&lt;p&gt;in knowledge economies, the prevailing belief is that research and innovation are crucial for&lt;br /&gt;
societal progress. the call for &lsquo;responsible&rsquo; research and innovation is, however, an&lt;br /&gt;
indication that the link between science and technology on the one hand and societal&lt;br /&gt;
progress on the other is less unequivocal than many would like to think. this article&lt;br /&gt;
wonders why the relationship between knowledge economies and &lsquo;responsible&rsquo; research and&lt;br /&gt;
innovation is not self-evident. it, consequently, proposes glocal sustainability research&lt;br /&gt;
(gsr) &ndash; and an appropriate process architecture for gsr &ndash; as a translation of responsible&lt;br /&gt;
research and innovation (rri) and it suggests a type of knowledge arena &ndash; and some&lt;br /&gt;
institutional preconditions for its institutionalisation &ndash; as a breeding ground for rri.&lt;/p&gt;
